  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nLow clouds and dense fog continue to affect Southwest MT this\nmorning. Increasing high clouds will prevent the dense fog from\nexpanding in areal coverage, but will also prevent the dense fog\nfrom burning off quickly this morning. Thus the no changes to the\ncurrent dense fog advisory statement. Otherwise, the main concern\nlater today/tonight will be the increasing chances for rain/snow\nas the next upper level trof approaches from the West. The\nheaviest precipitation should fall in the mountains with this\nsystem, with the southwest mountains being the favored area. Thus\na winter weather advisory has been issued for tonight for the\nsouthwest mountains around Pony, Big Sky and West Yellowstone.\n\nThis will be a quick moving trof, with most of the precipitation\nending by early Tuesday morning. For the rest of Tue/Wed, the\nairmass will remain a bit unstable for a few passing light\nrain/snow showers, but most/if any of the precipitation that falls\nshould be in the mountains.\n\nFor later in the work week...mostly Thu into Fri...an upper level\nlow will develop off the coast of the Pacific Northwest, but\ngenerally stall over that region. This will result in a moist\nsouthwesterly flow aloft developing over MT, which will allow for\nscattered rain/snow showers to move through the CWA from time to\ntime. The upper level trof will begin to move slowly eastward over\nthe weekend, and then moving across MT early next week. Thus the\nchances for light rain/snow showers will continue for several\ndays.\n\nOverall, afternoon temperatures will generally be a bit below\nnormal across the CWA, with a few locations possibly seeing near\nnormal temperatures Tue/Wed.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nThe main concern tonight will be the light snow across Southwest\nMT. Overall snow accumulations look to be on the lighter side,\nthus a winter weather advisory has been issued for one mountain\nzone...330...for this system. Rain is likely to change to snow at\nall elevations by Tue morning, but any snow accumulations at lower\nelevations generally look to be less than an inch at this time.\n\nPeriods of light snow are expected in the mountains later this\nweek and into this weekend. Overall most of the snow accumulations\nwill continue to be on the light side, but a winter statement\nmight be needed from time to time over portions of the CWA later\nthis week and into this weekend. Brusda",
